{
  "slug": "maximum-number-of-events-that-can-be-attended",
  "title": "Maximum Number of Events That Can Be Attended",
  "topic": "greedy",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "greedy day sweep with a min-heap",
  "statement": "You are given a list of events. Every event is a row of day numbers, and the event at position i can be attended on any single day `d` satisfying `events[i][0] <= d <= events[i][1]`. A row holding only one day number is a single-day event on that day, numbers after the second one in a row are ignored, and a row whose first number is greater than its second allows no day at all and cannot be attended. On any one day you may attend at most one event, and each event may be attended at most once. Return the maximum number of events you can attend, or 0 when the list is empty.\n\nFor `events = [[1, 2], [2, 3], [3, 4]]` you can attend the three events on days 1, 2 and 3. For `events = [[1, 1], [1, 1], [1, 1]]` only one of the three events fits on the single day they all allow, so the answer is 1.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= events.length <= 100`, `1 <= events[i] <= 100` and each event row holds at least one day number. Every day number in the list lies between 1 and 100, and a row may hold one, two or more day numbers.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "events = [[1, 1]]",
      "output": "1",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "events = [[7, 75], [62, 65]]",
      "output": "2",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(events: number[][])`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Looking only at when events start tells you nothing about which one to attend; the binding constraint is when they expire.",
    "Suppose several events are available today. Attending one of them uses up the day, so think about which choice keeps tomorrow's options widest.",
    "Sort by start day, add end days to a min-heap as their events become available, drop the ones that already expired, and always attend the earliest deadline."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The brute force walks the days from the earliest start to the latest end and, on each day, re-scans the entire event list to pick the still-unused event that allows today and ends earliest, then marks it used. With D days in the covered range and n events that is O(D*n) time and O(n) space, which is fine here because every day value is at most 100. The optimal makes the same choice without ever re-scanning: sort the events by start day, keep a min-heap of end days for the events whose start day has already been reached, and sweep a day pointer forward; on each day push every event that has now started, discard the events whose end day has already passed, and if the heap is still non-empty attend the event with the smallest end day, increment the count and advance the day.",
    "why_optimal": "Each event is pushed and popped at most once, and the sort is O(n log n), so the total work is O(n log n) with O(n) space, independent of how far apart the day values are. The greedy choice itself is forced: among the events available today, attending the one that expires earliest leaves a remaining set of deadlines that is no tighter than any other choice, so an exchange argument shows no schedule can do better. Days on which nothing can be attended are skipped by jumping the pointer to the next start day, which is why the number of day steps also stays O(n) instead of growing with the day range.",
    "pitfalls": "Comparing the heap front with `< day` leaves expired events available and over-counts; the same mistake one off in the other direction throws away events that can still be attended today. When the heap is empty the day pointer must jump to the next event's start day, otherwise the loop stalls or walks empty stretches. Re-scanning with the smallest *start* day instead of the smallest *end* day is the classic wrong greedy and fails on nested intervals such as `[[1,5], [1,1], [5,5]]`. A row is not guaranteed to be a pair: a row holding one day number is a one-day event on that day, and a row whose first number is greater than its second allows no day, so both shapes have to be normalised before the sweep. The day pointer must start at the earliest start day rather than at 0, or any event that closes on or before day 0 is silently dropped. Events with the same end day may be taken in any order, since only the count matters."
  },
